OpEx model is,

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:20.740
in a traditional
environment, you

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:25.550
have your budgeting cycle done
once a year, or once a quarter,

00:01:25.550 --> 00:01:26.050
right?

00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:27.730
But in an OpEx
environment, you have

00:01:27.730 --> 00:01:29.390
to do it on an ongoing basis.

00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:33.220
So you need a solution
that can do that for you.

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:36.097
So just look at why
it is important,

00:01:36.097 --> 00:01:37.180
the cloud cost management.

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:39.395
And you decided to
attend this session.

00:01:39.395 --> 00:01:41.270
That means you understand
why it's important.

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:44.620
But still, let's go through
some of the analyst research

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:45.710
on this one.

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:48.820
So when asked about, what
is your greatest barrier

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:51.250
for cloud adoption,
the second greatest

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:54.220
barrier was cited as the
cloud cost management,

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:55.880
just only after cloud--

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:59.590
the data security, which can
be-- which is the number one

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:01.310
greatest barrier.

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:04.370
And within cloud cost
management, when asked,

00:02:04.370 --> 00:02:07.120
what is your top pain--

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:13.300
so 61% of people said that the
predictability of your cloud

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:16.850
cost is the greatest
pain point for them.

00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:19.480
And what happens if you don't
have a effective cloud cost

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:21.920
management solution?

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:29.660
So 69% would overspend up to
25% of their cloud budget.

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:32.120
So this is a significant number.

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:36.580
And if you don't have a plan
for cloud cost management,

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:39.640
you would overspend more
than 70% of your budget.

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:41.040
So these are really big numbers.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:44.530
So it's still important to
have a cost management plan

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:48.400
and have a cost management
solution in place.

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:52.020
So now that we know why cloud
cost management is important,

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:55.600
you would want to start-- what
your solution has to answer.

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:58.920
So what are the questions you'd
want to answer using your cloud

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:00.668
cost management solution.

00:03:00.668 --> 00:03:02.460
So the first important
fundamental question

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:05.820
is, how much does your app cost?

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:08.010
And depending on at what
level you are looking at,

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:09.800
it can be one app,
a bunch of apps,

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:11.868
or all of apps in
your organization.

00:03:11.868 --> 00:03:13.410
So you would want
to know how much it

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:16.110
takes for you to deliver
value to your business

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:18.220
through your apps.

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:22.823
Second, for number two question,
is, what are your cost trends?

00:03:22.823 --> 00:03:24.240
You can be in a
seasonal variation

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:26.070
industry or a regular industry.

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:28.480
But you should know,
what area your trends,

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:31.950
overall and also
for individual apps.

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:34.710
And finally, what are
your cost drivers?

00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:37.500
So what is driving your cost?

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:39.180
What are your top
five spend areas.

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:41.050
How would you drill
down into that?

00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:43.245
So that's another
important question.

00:03:43.245 --> 00:03:45.120
Even though these are
fundamental questions--

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:48.390
and Google Cloud provides a
lot of solutions out of the box

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:52.290
that can answer these questions
for you in a matter of minutes,

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:54.860
that Stephen will
showcase later on--

00:03:54.860 --> 00:03:56.340
but these are not
all the questions

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:57.210
that you need to answer.

00:03:57.210 --> 00:03:58.710
These are the
fundamental questions,

00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:00.510
but based on your
organization, you

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:04.260
may have much more--
many more questions.

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:07.440
So let's look at
one example where

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:12.240
we help build a billing data
analysis solution for a Fortune

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:12.795
500 retailer.

00:04:15.850 --> 00:04:19.329
So just give a quick
background on this customer--

00:04:19.329 --> 00:04:23.100
so a couple of years ago, they
started on a really big cloud

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:26.160
transmission journey using GCP.

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:29.110
We helped build a
business case for them.

00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:31.260
And it was a very
exhaustive business case

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:33.780
in terms of what
initiatives, milestones,

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:36.990
going down all the way
to the applications.

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:39.780
And very early on
in the journey,

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:42.760
we realized the importance of
cloud cost management solution.

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:45.690
And as we were getting ready,
building the foundation,

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:47.940
getting ready for the
bigger transformation,

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:50.310
we also started building
the solution required

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:56.650
for cloud billing data
analysis and reporting.

00:04:56.650 --> 00:04:59.100
So as part of the cloud
cost management team,

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:03.090
we were responsible for
oversight analysis reporting

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:05.760
for all aspects of
cloud cost management.

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:10.617
So we needed a system which
can meet all these challenges.

00:05:10.617 --> 00:05:13.200
So the first set of challenges
were organizational challenges.

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:16.140
And I'm sure all bigger
organizations would really

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:17.440
face this.

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:22.270
So every organization will
have some kind of a structure.

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:25.680
For example, they'll have lines
of businesses, vice presidents,

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:28.630
departments, applications,
and stuff like that.

00:05:28.630 --> 00:05:32.010
So how will you overlay all of
this data, your organization

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:34.260
structure, onto
your GCP billing?

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:36.450
Because you may have to
help showcase all of that

00:05:36.450 --> 00:05:39.480
back using that structure.

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:42.060
And second option
was, how will you

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:43.960
combine multiple data sources?

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:45.990
So GCP Billing is
not the only source

00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:47.693
of cost in your overall system.

00:05:47.693 --> 00:05:49.110
So there are so
many other systems

00:05:49.110 --> 00:05:52.680
like licensing, other
third-party tools, other colo

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:54.627
and other cloud
providers as well.

00:05:54.627 --> 00:05:56.960
So how will you combine all
those multiple data sources?

00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:58.950
So your solution has
to be robust enough

00:05:58.950 --> 00:06:01.290
to combine all of these
sources and derive

00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:04.150
meaningful insights from that.

00:06:04.150 --> 00:06:06.828
And finally, in terms
of organization,

00:06:06.828 --> 00:06:08.620
there are different
levels of stakeholders.

00:06:08.620 --> 00:06:12.310
So how will you break down the
reporting or analysis based

00:06:12.310 --> 00:06:13.780
on different stakeholders?

00:06:13.780 --> 00:06:15.597
And how will you
control access, right?

00:06:15.597 --> 00:06:17.680
You wouldn't want application--
line of business 1

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:21.000
to look at line of
business 2 data like that.

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:25.240
And in terms of data challenges,
working with billing export

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:27.400
schema is really important.

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:30.370
So sooner your team is very
familiar with the billing

00:06:30.370 --> 00:06:32.720
export schema and
overall BigQuery,

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:35.530
it's really important.

00:06:35.530 --> 00:06:37.720
And how would you distribute
shared services, right?

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:42.190
So there can be shared services
like networking, monitoring.

00:06:42.190 --> 00:06:44.150
So how will you
distribute that cost?

00:06:44.150 --> 00:06:45.910
And some of the
credits that come,

00:06:45.910 --> 00:06:47.800
they come at the
organization level.

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:49.810
How will you distribute
those credits back

00:06:49.810 --> 00:06:52.270
to the respective folks?

00:06:52.270 --> 00:06:54.790
And finally, every
large organization

00:06:54.790 --> 00:06:58.810
would have some kind of a data
analysis reporting solution

00:06:58.810 --> 00:07:01.550
in terms of cost
already existing.

00:07:01.550 --> 00:07:06.550
So whether you will choose to
extend that solution for GCP,

00:07:06.550 --> 00:07:08.830
or you will want to build
a brand new one-- so that's

00:07:08.830 --> 00:07:11.250
another question
you need to answer.

00:07:14.470 --> 00:07:16.623
So with these
challenges in mind,

00:07:16.623 --> 00:07:18.040
this is how we
came up with-- this

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:21.040
is a overview of the
architecture that we build.

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:23.010
So if you see, on
the left-hand side,

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:25.050
there are a bunch
of data sources.

00:07:25.050 --> 00:07:28.680
Of course, GCP Billing export,
that was a major data source.

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:31.650
And as a best practice,
we have to enable--

00:07:31.650 --> 00:07:36.480
you should enable GCP
export on day one.

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:38.200
And then there are
other GCP data.

00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:42.260
For example, we actually had
a lot of other requirements

00:07:42.260 --> 00:07:44.670
in terms of getting
more data from GCP--

00:07:44.670 --> 00:07:46.160
for example, how
many instances are

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:47.710
running in our environment--

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:49.690
so any other
platform-related data.

00:07:49.690 --> 00:07:52.170
So we had custom scripts
to capture all of that data

00:07:52.170 --> 00:07:54.510
and use--

00:07:54.510 --> 00:07:56.940
dump into BigQuery.

00:07:56.940 --> 00:07:59.070
And then organizational
data, right-- all

00:07:59.070 --> 00:08:03.460
of your org structure,
your VPs, your departments,

00:08:03.460 --> 00:08:06.240
your budgets, all of that
organizational data has

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:09.780
to be brought in as well
for meaningful insights.

00:08:09.780 --> 00:08:12.540
And we'll see that
in the demo later on.

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:14.040
You can have
third-party tools data.

00:08:14.040 --> 00:08:17.520
And you can have other clouds,
or colo-provided data as well.

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:19.530
You would want one
single solution

00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:21.300
to do-- to visualize
all of that,

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:26.020
right, instead of going to
multiple different solutions.

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:30.490
For storage and analysis,
we used BigQuery.

00:08:30.490 --> 00:08:32.630
Initially, we started off
with a on-prem solution,

00:08:32.630 --> 00:08:35.473
but later on,
realized, related to--

00:08:35.473 --> 00:08:37.390
challenges related to
cost and functionality--

00:08:37.390 --> 00:08:40.780
we decided to get everything
into Google Cloud--

00:08:40.780 --> 00:08:45.040
so using BigQuery as our
storage and analysis solution.

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:46.500
And we'll see some
of the example

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:50.340
in our demo, how we
use that actually.

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:52.460
And we had a bunch of
automation and scheduling.

00:08:52.460 --> 00:08:54.770
Because all of the org
data that you see here,

00:08:54.770 --> 00:08:57.440
all the platform data that
is coming, all of that data

00:08:57.440 --> 00:08:59.030
was automated.

00:08:59.030 --> 00:09:02.408
So there were scripts running
all the time, scheduled.

00:09:02.408 --> 00:09:04.700
And there were third-party--
or the organizational data

00:09:04.700 --> 00:09:05.960
coming into CSV files.

00:09:05.960 --> 00:09:08.420
And whenever there was
a change in the file,

00:09:08.420 --> 00:09:11.683
the automation pipeline
would update the table.

00:09:11.683 --> 00:09:13.350
And finally, we used
a bunch of BigQuery

00:09:13.350 --> 00:09:16.490
views to join all of these
different data sources.

00:09:16.490 --> 00:09:18.800
And for visualization,
we have used Tableau,

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:21.290
because that was
the tool of choice.

00:09:25.930 --> 00:09:27.630
So before I go to
the next one, I just

00:09:27.630 --> 00:09:31.810
want to highlight a couple of
key highlights of the solution.

00:09:31.810 --> 00:09:35.310
So far cost organizations,
we used labels.

00:09:35.310 --> 00:09:38.700
So we decided a labeling
strategy very early on.

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:41.760
And as a best practice, you
should spend time on deciding

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:44.410
the labeling strategy,
and not just for today's--

00:09:44.410 --> 00:09:46.765
well near future, maybe
slightly longer than that.

00:09:46.765 --> 00:09:48.390
Because it's really
hard to change once

00:09:48.390 --> 00:09:50.490
you decide on a
labeling strategy.

00:09:50.490 --> 00:09:52.320
That was one important part.

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:54.240
And because of that
labeling strategy,

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:57.210
we were able to drill
down not just by the VP,

00:09:57.210 --> 00:09:59.370
but going down to
the department,

00:09:59.370 --> 00:10:02.010
going down to the application,
going down to the environment.

00:10:02.010 --> 00:10:05.790
So there was visibility going
across, all the way down.

00:10:05.790 --> 00:10:07.310
Second interesting part.

00:10:07.310 --> 00:10:12.260
We did was we used billing
export data merged with so

00:10:12.260 --> 00:10:13.660
many other data sources.

00:10:13.660 --> 00:10:17.080
And we'll see how that is
useful later on in the demo.

00:10:17.080 --> 00:10:19.780
So don't be afraid to
really merge different data

00:10:19.780 --> 00:10:23.110
sources with your billing
to get more insights.

00:10:23.110 --> 00:10:26.120
And finally, we create a lot
of custom fields as well.

00:10:26.120 --> 00:10:28.240
And we'll see one
example of that.

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:32.050
If you have seen your BigQuery
export, for every service,

00:10:32.050 --> 00:10:33.990
you have multiple SKUs.

00:10:33.990 --> 00:10:37.300
And for computing, for example,
there are thousands of SKUs.

00:10:37.300 --> 00:10:40.690
So if you wanted to jump
from service to SKU,

00:10:40.690 --> 00:10:42.400
that's a much bigger jump.

00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:45.820
So we came up with a field
called SKU categories.

00:10:45.820 --> 00:10:48.690
So we wanted to categorize some
of the SKUs into something more

00:10:48.690 --> 00:10:50.065
higher-level and
more meaningful.

00:10:50.065 --> 00:10:54.100
' Because not everybody would
want to go to the actual SKUs

00:10:54.100 --> 00:10:55.280
later on.

00:10:55.280 --> 00:10:57.250
And we'll see how that
helps later on as well.

00:11:01.930 --> 00:11:05.210
And in terms of impact--

00:11:05.210 --> 00:11:08.590
so the major impact was the
visibility from the get-go.

00:11:08.590 --> 00:11:13.570
So we could track the spend on
a overall business case level.

00:11:13.570 --> 00:11:16.690
And the leadership could
see, now, how they are doing

00:11:16.690 --> 00:11:18.040
and make adjustments.

00:11:18.040 --> 00:11:20.560
So this was a great tool
to the overall leadership,

00:11:20.560 --> 00:11:24.340
to look at it and see how well
we are performing vis-a-vis

00:11:24.340 --> 00:11:26.680
the business case.

00:11:26.680 --> 00:11:29.170
And not just that--
because the reports were

00:11:29.170 --> 00:11:33.700
available all the way down to
the app owners and developers,

00:11:33.700 --> 00:11:36.790
it really helped make that
mindset change from CapEx

00:11:36.790 --> 00:11:38.650
to OpEx.

00:11:38.650 --> 00:11:40.970
As the granular-level
data was available,

00:11:40.970 --> 00:11:43.660
they could see impact
of their actions.

00:11:43.660 --> 00:11:46.810
If they design an
application in a certain way,

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:49.240
they could see a positive or
negative impact on the cost

00:11:49.240 --> 00:11:51.310
immediately in near real terms.

00:11:51.310 --> 00:11:55.350
So that really helped create
the CapEx to OpEx mindset.

00:11:55.350 --> 00:11:57.460
And in terms of analysis,
because everybody

00:11:57.460 --> 00:12:01.480
could see what their budget is
and how their spend is going

00:12:01.480 --> 00:12:04.210
on a daily, monthly,
weekly basis,

00:12:04.210 --> 00:12:06.250
it created a culture
of accountability

00:12:06.250 --> 00:12:08.700
across the organization.

00:12:08.700 --> 00:12:12.430
And as everybody could see, what
are their top areas of spend,

00:12:12.430 --> 00:12:15.790
they could manage what are
their top areas of spend,

00:12:15.790 --> 00:12:19.600
do something about it,
and kind of control that.

00:12:19.600 --> 00:12:22.338
And overall, it helped
improve the forecasting

00:12:22.338 --> 00:12:23.130
and predictability.

00:12:23.130 --> 00:12:26.520
Because everybody was able to
see how much their application

00:12:26.520 --> 00:12:29.700
cost yesterday, today,
and based on the load

00:12:29.700 --> 00:12:32.760
variations, what it
will cost tomorrow.

00:12:32.760 --> 00:12:35.400
And finally, in
terms of insights,

00:12:35.400 --> 00:12:40.010
it helped identify a lot of
optimization opportunities.

00:12:40.010 --> 00:12:42.570
And couple of
examples I can give--

00:12:42.570 --> 00:12:45.030
when we realized the compute
cost was much higher,

00:12:45.030 --> 00:12:47.100
we'd dig deeper into that.

00:12:47.100 --> 00:12:50.650
We saw, in one instance, the
licensing cost was much higher.

00:12:50.650 --> 00:12:54.070
So we decided to look
at, why is it higher.

00:12:54.070 --> 00:12:56.700
So is it-- is there some way
that we can address that?

00:12:56.700 --> 00:12:59.910
Maybe using BYOL, right,
we can reduce the cost.

00:12:59.910 --> 00:13:01.320
Other examples
were we identified

00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:04.620
a lot of storage-related
costs in compute.

00:13:04.620 --> 00:13:07.410
And then that's where the
custom scripts came in handy.

00:13:07.410 --> 00:13:09.810
Because we wrote
custom scripts that

00:13:09.810 --> 00:13:11.780
could go through
all of your projects

00:13:11.780 --> 00:13:14.730
and find out orphan disks,
that disks which are not

00:13:14.730 --> 00:13:16.530
attached to any instance.

00:13:16.530 --> 00:13:19.240
So you're paying for storage
for those instances--

00:13:19.240 --> 00:13:21.520
those disks-- and
not using them.

00:13:21.520 --> 00:13:23.910
So that is-- that was
another opportunity

00:13:23.910 --> 00:13:28.000
to delete, or take a snapshot,
or do something about that.

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:33.690
And finally, other opportunities
in terms of making this

00:13:33.690 --> 00:13:34.770
as part of your culture--

00:13:34.770 --> 00:13:37.830
the cost optimization is
becoming part of your culture.

00:13:37.830 --> 00:13:40.680
And to do that, we
had regular governance

00:13:40.680 --> 00:13:43.620
meetings with our
lines of businesses

00:13:43.620 --> 00:13:46.390
on trends and recommendations.

00:13:46.390 --> 00:13:50.280
So rather than making
it once a quarter

00:13:50.280 --> 00:13:53.640
or a long-term activity, we
made it a habit, our governance,

00:13:53.640 --> 00:13:56.010
to do it, like, biweekly basis.

00:13:56.010 --> 00:13:58.260
And one of the
interesting anecdote

00:13:58.260 --> 00:13:59.850
I remember from one
of such meetings

00:13:59.850 --> 00:14:03.900
was when we were helping
out predicting one--

00:14:03.900 --> 00:14:06.740
how to predict the cost
for one of the LoBs.

00:14:06.740 --> 00:14:09.468
We realized, for a peak--

00:14:09.468 --> 00:14:11.010
because it's a retail
customer, there

00:14:11.010 --> 00:14:15.250
were peaks and troughs
for their usage.

00:14:15.250 --> 00:14:17.970
And we were trying to
estimate how much it will

00:14:17.970 --> 00:14:20.370
cost when it goes on a peak.

00:14:20.370 --> 00:14:25.290
So obviously, we had a unit
cost on a traditional basis.

00:14:25.290 --> 00:14:30.180
But during a peak, your overall
on-demand usage goes up, right?

00:14:30.180 --> 00:14:33.150
So you have certain
commitments for a lower cost,

00:14:33.150 --> 00:14:36.330
but you cannot make commitments
for a short period of peak.

00:14:36.330 --> 00:14:39.360
So during the peak, your
on-demand instance usage

00:14:39.360 --> 00:14:41.000
goes up.

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:42.930
And that actually
pushes your unit price

00:14:42.930 --> 00:14:45.390
for compute up higher as well.

00:14:45.390 --> 00:14:47.760
So when predicting for
a peak, you not just

00:14:47.760 --> 00:14:50.620
have to predict for
the increased capacity,

00:14:50.620 --> 00:14:52.320
you have to predict--

00:14:52.320 --> 00:14:55.660
use the increased
unit cost as well.

00:14:55.660 --> 00:14:57.000
Keep in mind that as well.

00:14:57.000 --> 00:14:59.490
So that insight was really
useful for some of the LoBs

00:14:59.490 --> 00:15:01.950
to make accurate
predictions about how

00:15:01.950 --> 00:15:04.210
the peak would look like.

00:15:04.210 --> 00:15:07.480
And finally, before closing
out about this use case,

00:15:07.480 --> 00:15:09.660
what are some of the key
takeaways that you can take

00:15:09.660 --> 00:15:13.390
back to your billing systems?

00:15:13.390 --> 00:15:15.870
So the first one
is billing analysis

00:15:15.870 --> 00:15:20.400
and reporting is not one and
done kind of solution, right?

00:15:20.400 --> 00:15:22.830
And we actually
developed this solution

00:15:22.830 --> 00:15:25.050
in a very agile,
iterative fashion.

00:15:25.050 --> 00:15:27.090
So don't try to collect
all your requirements

00:15:27.090 --> 00:15:31.350
for all your dashboards
very early in the cycle.

00:15:31.350 --> 00:15:32.820
You should start
building reports

00:15:32.820 --> 00:15:35.920
and start improving on
that on a biweekly basis.

00:15:35.920 --> 00:15:37.640
So if you do it in
an iterative fashion,

00:15:37.640 --> 00:15:40.210
that's the best way to do it.

00:15:40.210 --> 00:15:42.810
The second one is, decide your
data pipeline and reporting

00:15:42.810 --> 00:15:44.150
tack early on.

00:15:44.150 --> 00:15:48.120
We had to make corrections,
adjustment in the midway.

00:15:48.120 --> 00:15:51.330
But always, you should
do some due diligence

00:15:51.330 --> 00:15:54.960
to decide your data
pipeline stack early on.

00:15:54.960 --> 00:15:57.540
And identifying
optimization opportunities

00:15:57.540 --> 00:16:00.660
should not be a
rarely occurring thing

00:16:00.660 --> 00:16:03.180
it should be happening
much more regularly.

00:16:03.180 --> 00:16:06.100
That way, you can
control your cost.

00:16:06.100 --> 00:16:09.150
So with that, I'll
hand it over to Stephen

00:16:09.150 --> 00:16:12.590
to talk about the cloud cost
management tools on GCP.

00:16:12.590 --> 00:16:14.670
STEPHEN ELLIOTT: All
right, thank you, Shashank.

00:16:14.670 --> 00:16:15.900
This is actually kind of
funny, because I never

00:16:15.900 --> 00:16:18.750
thought I would get to present
Billing in an IMAX theater.

00:16:18.750 --> 00:16:20.340
So it's an exciting
day, because I

00:16:20.340 --> 00:16:22.590
get to take check that
off my thrilling bucket

00:16:22.590 --> 00:16:24.400
list of life goals.

00:16:24.400 --> 00:16:28.230
So we talked to lots of
customers, big and small.

00:16:28.230 --> 00:16:30.500
And there's several key
themes-- and Shashank kind of

00:16:30.500 --> 00:16:32.250
alluded to these--
that we want to address

00:16:32.250 --> 00:16:34.435
with our cost management tools.

00:16:34.435 --> 00:16:36.310
And these are what's on
the screen right now.

00:16:36.310 --> 00:16:38.190
So visibility--
the basic ability

00:16:38.190 --> 00:16:42.420
to view your costs in a way
that's meaningful to you.

00:16:42.420 --> 00:16:45.132
Accountability-- so once
you know the cost of some

00:16:45.132 --> 00:16:46.590
meaningful, logical
grouping-- say,

00:16:46.590 --> 00:16:49.090
the fully-loaded cost of the
application I just shipped last

00:16:49.090 --> 00:16:50.040
week--

00:16:50.040 --> 00:16:52.507
now, who do I hold
accountable for the cost

00:16:52.507 --> 00:16:53.340
of that application?

00:16:53.340 --> 00:16:57.330
Who do I reward for having
delivered it efficiently?

00:16:57.330 --> 00:16:59.310
And who do I have
a meeting with who

00:16:59.310 --> 00:17:00.690
did not deliver it efficiently?

00:17:00.690 --> 00:17:03.220
But more seriously, what
is the corrective action

00:17:03.220 --> 00:17:06.032
if you see costs
starting to run away?

00:17:06.032 --> 00:17:07.740
And then control--
now this is a big one,

00:17:07.740 --> 00:17:11.160
because there's both the
proactive, explicit control,

00:17:11.160 --> 00:17:13.388
and the tools that you
use to control costs,

00:17:13.388 --> 00:17:15.180
and then there's just
the sense of control.

00:17:15.180 --> 00:17:16.980
And that one's hard
to put a finger on.

00:17:16.980 --> 00:17:18.980
But often, a bunch
of different tools

00:17:18.980 --> 00:17:21.680
can give you the sense and the
feeling of being in control.

00:17:21.680 --> 00:17:25.079
That's often just as important
for you and your leadership.

00:17:25.079 --> 00:17:27.230
You know, if you're
a CIO, or a VP,

00:17:27.230 --> 00:17:28.605
or you're an
engineering manager,

00:17:28.605 --> 00:17:30.105
it's that sense of
control that lets

00:17:30.105 --> 00:17:32.370
you be confident that you're
doing the right thing.

00:17:32.370 --> 00:17:35.270
And then intelligence-- so
you expect to get insights

00:17:35.270 --> 00:17:40.010
about your costs, suggestions
about where you can optimize

00:17:40.010 --> 00:17:42.553
what you're spending, and
get those from the platform,

00:17:42.553 --> 00:17:43.970
and get that easily
from the data.

00:17:43.970 --> 00:17:48.770
So those are the four
priorities we have on Billing.

00:17:48.770 --> 00:17:51.152
And I'm going to talk
through the tools,

00:17:51.152 --> 00:17:53.360
and then show you the tools,
and then demo the tools.

00:17:53.360 --> 00:17:55.610
So there'll be three
different ways depending

00:17:55.610 --> 00:17:57.170
what kind of learner you are.

00:17:57.170 --> 00:18:00.020
So we have built-in reports that
come straight out of the box.

00:18:00.020 --> 00:18:03.770
They're ready to slice and
dice in the Billing console.

00:18:03.770 --> 00:18:06.015
If you want to get access
to the detailed data,

00:18:06.015 --> 00:18:08.390
we export all the detailed
data, like Shashank mentioned,

00:18:08.390 --> 00:18:11.540
to BigQuery, which is
a really powerful tool

00:18:11.540 --> 00:18:15.692
to get really fast query results
on large amounts of data.

00:18:15.692 --> 00:18:17.150
So you won't be
limited by BigQuery

00:18:17.150 --> 00:18:20.420
if you want to join it
against rich operational data.

00:18:20.420 --> 00:18:21.480
We have recommendations.

00:18:21.480 --> 00:18:23.605
So various products have
different recommendations,

00:18:23.605 --> 00:18:24.720
for example.

00:18:24.720 --> 00:18:26.958
Compute Engine has VM
rightsizing recommendations

00:18:26.958 --> 00:18:29.000
to show you where you
might want to have a larger

00:18:29.000 --> 00:18:31.190
instance, or a smaller
instance, depending

00:18:31.190 --> 00:18:35.930
on how you're actually using the
cores and memory in your VMs.

00:18:35.930 --> 00:18:37.350
And then customizable
dashboards--

00:18:37.350 --> 00:18:40.460
so this would be, in the
case of a Google product,

00:18:40.460 --> 00:18:44.117
Data Studio plugged
into BigQuery.

00:18:44.117 --> 00:18:45.950
Or if you have your own
solution-- you know,

00:18:45.950 --> 00:18:48.620
you're using Tableau, or Looker,
or some other solution-- plug

00:18:48.620 --> 00:18:51.850
that into BigQuery as well.

00:18:51.850 --> 00:18:54.700
On the accountability and
control side of the house,

00:18:54.700 --> 00:18:56.500
again, this is all
about giving you

00:18:56.500 --> 00:18:58.990
the tools you need to feel
like you are in control,

00:18:58.990 --> 00:19:01.930
and can see costs, and
don't get surprised by costs

00:19:01.930 --> 00:19:06.020
as your teams are iterating and
kind of operating autonomously.

00:19:06.020 --> 00:19:08.230
So one is just billing
access control,

00:19:08.230 --> 00:19:12.700
being able to set the rights to
view costs for the right users.

00:19:12.700 --> 00:19:15.640
By default, billing account
administrators and project

00:19:15.640 --> 00:19:18.688
owners and users get to see the
costs of what they're doing.

00:19:18.688 --> 00:19:20.730
Then we have budget alerts
and automated actions.

00:19:20.730 --> 00:19:23.410
So you can set
notification thresholds

00:19:23.410 --> 00:19:26.380
on your spending in a project
or in a billing account

00:19:26.380 --> 00:19:29.350
so that you receive an email if
spending goes over a threshold.

00:19:29.350 --> 00:19:31.510
Or you get a
Pub/Sub message sent

00:19:31.510 --> 00:19:34.390
to some programmatic
function that responds to it

00:19:34.390 --> 00:19:37.950
and takes corrective action.

00:19:37.950 --> 00:19:39.690
And then there's the
resource hierarchy.

00:19:39.690 --> 00:19:42.410
So this is just how
you want to organize

00:19:42.410 --> 00:19:44.840
your resources, your
usage into projects,

00:19:44.840 --> 00:19:46.580
have multiple projects
under a folder,

00:19:46.580 --> 00:19:48.530
have all of that
under your domain,

00:19:48.530 --> 00:19:51.590
and then set standard
policies at the right level

00:19:51.590 --> 00:19:52.818
of the hierarchy.

00:19:52.818 --> 00:19:54.360
And then finally
here, there's quota.

00:19:54.360 --> 00:19:57.080
And so one way to
think about quota

00:19:57.080 --> 00:20:01.700
is the proactive control of
usage, setting, in advance,

00:20:01.700 --> 00:20:03.228
what someone's allowed to use.

00:20:03.228 --> 00:20:05.270
And then budgets and
alerts, that's notification.

00:20:05.270 --> 00:20:08.210
So that's a lagging indicator to
respond to things after they've

00:20:08.210 --> 00:20:12.150
exceeded a certain threshold.

00:20:12.150 --> 00:20:15.900
So this graph makes me laugh,
just because I stubbornly

00:20:15.900 --> 00:20:18.990
oversimplify it despite
the most vigorous feedback

00:20:18.990 --> 00:20:20.460
from my engineers.

00:20:20.460 --> 00:20:24.705
So on the left, there's usage
metering from GCP products.

00:20:24.705 --> 00:20:26.580
So if you're using
Compute Engine or Storage,

00:20:26.580 --> 00:20:30.360
it's emitting usage to what
I've called, here, the pricing

00:20:30.360 --> 00:20:32.260
engine, which doesn't exist.

00:20:32.260 --> 00:20:33.450
This is a chimera.

00:20:33.450 --> 00:20:34.950
It's not a real thing.

00:20:34.950 --> 00:20:37.388
But it's a really complex
system that takes the usage,

00:20:37.388 --> 00:20:39.180
figures out the right
pricing, and credits,

00:20:39.180 --> 00:20:41.940
and discounts to apply
to it, and packages that

00:20:41.940 --> 00:20:44.190
to send to downstream systems.

00:20:44.190 --> 00:20:45.700
So one is important to us.

00:20:45.700 --> 00:20:46.590
That's payments.

00:20:46.590 --> 00:20:48.090
That's how we make money.

00:20:48.090 --> 00:20:51.092
But for this talk, the yellow
items and the red items

00:20:51.092 --> 00:20:52.050
are what are important.

00:20:52.050 --> 00:20:53.820
So we send the
cost and the usage

00:20:53.820 --> 00:20:57.060
downstream to all the
cost management tools--

00:20:57.060 --> 00:21:00.240
billing reports,
export, and dashboards,

00:21:00.240 --> 00:21:01.957
and budgets and alerts.

00:21:01.957 --> 00:21:04.290
So if you run into Jonathan
Thorndycraft or anyone else,

00:21:04.290 --> 00:21:06.450
and you talk to them, just
don't mention the word "pricing

00:21:06.450 --> 00:21:06.690
engine."

00:21:06.690 --> 00:21:07.773
Talk about something else.

00:21:07.773 --> 00:21:10.833
But this is basically
what it does.

00:21:10.833 --> 00:21:13.250
There's a great talk about
resource management-- actually,

00:21:13.250 --> 00:21:15.860
later today, I'll give
you the talk number--

00:21:15.860 --> 00:21:18.110
by Greg and Max, teammates.

00:21:18.110 --> 00:21:21.200
But at a high level, the items
that you should think about

00:21:21.200 --> 00:21:23.690
are, how do you set policies
and users in your domain

00:21:23.690 --> 00:21:25.200
and organization?

00:21:25.200 --> 00:21:27.232
And then, how do you
pay for your projects

00:21:27.232 --> 00:21:28.190
with a billing account?

00:21:28.190 --> 00:21:30.232
Usually, most customers
have one billing account,

00:21:30.232 --> 00:21:32.600
so this shouldn't
require too much thought.

00:21:32.600 --> 00:21:35.060
The payment profile-- just
something to be aware of

00:21:35.060 --> 00:21:37.227
and make sure you have the
right users set on those,

00:21:37.227 --> 00:21:38.900
so they receive the
invoices and they're

00:21:38.900 --> 00:21:42.470
able to pay for the invoice.

00:21:42.470 --> 00:21:44.150
And then projects,
folders, and labels,

00:21:44.150 --> 00:21:46.140
which Shashank referred
to and I mentioned,

00:21:46.140 --> 00:21:48.057
which are the ways to
organize their resources

00:21:48.057 --> 00:21:50.690
and be able to identify
costs of the same group--

00:21:54.180 --> 00:21:55.775
So this is the
part where I'm just

00:21:55.775 --> 00:21:58.400
going to show you what all these
things are before I demo them.

00:21:58.400 --> 00:22:00.028
So reports look like this.

00:22:00.028 --> 00:22:01.070
They work out of the box.

00:22:01.070 --> 00:22:02.530
You don't have to
set anything up.

00:22:02.530 --> 00:22:04.250
And anytime you
log in, you can see

00:22:04.250 --> 00:22:07.340
a report that lets you
slice and dice the costs

00:22:07.340 --> 00:22:09.780
for easy visibility.

00:22:09.780 --> 00:22:12.020
And then billing export--
if you go to BigQuery

00:22:12.020 --> 00:22:13.520
and you have billing
export enabled,

00:22:13.520 --> 00:22:16.070
you can just look at the table
where all the costs are going

00:22:16.070 --> 00:22:17.487
and either run the
queries in line

00:22:17.487 --> 00:22:20.960
here, run the query via a
command line, or via an API,

00:22:20.960 --> 00:22:22.970
or plug it into Data
Studio or Tableau,

00:22:22.970 --> 00:22:25.220
and then maybe have an
experience where you don't even

00:22:25.220 --> 00:22:26.220
need to write the query.

00:22:26.220 --> 00:22:27.950
For example, in Data
Studio, you can just

00:22:27.950 --> 00:22:30.080
do a what you see is what
you get configuration

00:22:30.080 --> 00:22:32.660
of the charts that pulls
directly from billing export

00:22:32.660 --> 00:22:34.430
and then gives you
something like this.

00:22:34.430 --> 00:22:35.630
And we actually
have a template--

00:22:35.630 --> 00:22:37.160
and Shashank will
show that in a demo--

00:22:37.160 --> 00:22:38.702
where you can just
copy the template,

00:22:38.702 --> 00:22:40.790
plug in your export
table, and then get

00:22:40.790 --> 00:22:45.270
it to start populating real
data from your own account.

00:22:45.270 --> 00:22:50.470
And budgets and alerts, this is
per billing account or project.

00:22:50.470 --> 00:22:53.220
And then you can easily set
multiple alert thresholds

00:22:53.220 --> 00:22:57.160
based on actual spending
level or a forecasted spending

00:22:57.160 --> 00:22:57.660
amount.

00:22:57.660 --> 00:22:58.702
And that's a new release.

00:22:58.702 --> 00:23:01.530
We came out with that
just a couple weeks ago.

00:23:01.530 --> 00:23:05.000
So that's a nice one
that I'll talk about.

00:23:05.000 --> 00:23:09.920
And then just the mental model
for how programmatic budget

00:23:09.920 --> 00:23:12.050
alerts and programmatic
consumption of those alerts

00:23:12.050 --> 00:23:12.830
work--

00:23:12.830 --> 00:23:14.330
you can think of--
the manual method

00:23:14.330 --> 00:23:16.705
is we send an email to the
billing administrator and say,

00:23:16.705 --> 00:23:19.520
your spending is
approaching your threshold.

00:23:19.520 --> 00:23:22.550
You might want to do something
about it, or just a heads-up.

00:23:22.550 --> 00:23:26.450
Programmatically, if we sent
the same message to a Pub/Sub

00:23:26.450 --> 00:23:28.850
topic, you could have
something like a Cloud Function

00:23:28.850 --> 00:23:31.462
in just the Pub/Sub
topic message anytime

00:23:31.462 --> 00:23:33.420
it arrives, and then
decide what to do with it.

00:23:33.420 --> 00:23:36.980
So for example, one solution
would be to just send a Slack

00:23:36.980 --> 00:23:41.780
message to a Slack group
and say, hey, just FYI,

00:23:41.780 --> 00:23:43.940
the budget's-- the
spending is trending up.

00:23:43.940 --> 00:23:45.740
Or if it's a sandbox
project and you're

00:23:45.740 --> 00:23:47.582
completely comfortable
blowing it away,

00:23:47.582 --> 00:23:49.790
you could have a more
draconian programmatic response

00:23:49.790 --> 00:23:51.990
this says, OK, you
spent more than $500.

00:23:51.990 --> 00:23:53.372
That was your test account.

00:23:53.372 --> 00:23:54.830
Your test account's
been shut down.

00:23:57.750 --> 00:24:00.755
And this is just kind of a
small pitch to Cloud Function.

00:24:00.755 --> 00:24:02.130
But when I say
it's easy to write

00:24:02.130 --> 00:24:05.100
the programmatic consumption
of the Pub/Sub message,

00:24:05.100 --> 00:24:07.610
Cloud Functions are
really short snippets

00:24:07.610 --> 00:24:09.160
of code in common languages.

00:24:09.160 --> 00:24:11.880
So here we have a
very short function

00:24:11.880 --> 00:24:15.095
that does a quick budget
check and then does--

00:24:15.095 --> 00:24:16.470
kind of pushes
the big red button

00:24:16.470 --> 00:24:20.290
and says disable billing,
because we exceeded the budget.

00:24:20.290 --> 00:24:23.220
So for budgets and quota,
again, keep in mind,

00:24:23.220 --> 00:24:25.800
budgets are your
lagging indicator.

00:24:25.800 --> 00:24:28.050
It's when the spending
has happened, which means

00:24:28.050 --> 00:24:29.610
the usage happened before that.

00:24:29.610 --> 00:24:31.950
And quota is your
proactive control.

00:24:31.950 --> 00:24:36.090
And the quota policies can be
set for different products.

00:24:36.090 --> 00:24:37.710
You can decide
whether or not a user

00:24:37.710 --> 00:24:39.335
should be able to
run a lot of BigQuery

00:24:39.335 --> 00:24:41.820
or run a lot of instances.

00:24:41.820 --> 00:24:44.500
And on budgets, that
is kind of a catch-all.

00:24:44.500 --> 00:24:46.830
So if the project
spends a certain level,

00:24:46.830 --> 00:24:50.027
that captures all the
products in that project.

00:24:50.027 --> 00:24:51.860
If a billing account
spends a certain level,

00:24:51.860 --> 00:24:54.012
that just captures all of
the spending and activity

00:24:54.012 --> 00:24:54.970
in the billing account.

00:24:54.970 --> 00:24:57.630
So quotas are also more
targeted to the actual products

00:24:57.630 --> 00:25:00.130
being used.

00:25:00.130 --> 00:25:03.820
So another important
aspect to cost management--

00:25:03.820 --> 00:25:05.690
and many of you may
be familiar with this.

00:25:05.690 --> 00:25:07.810
But if you get
into a good rhythm

00:25:07.810 --> 00:25:11.110
of building the dashboards,
as Shashank mentioned,

00:25:11.110 --> 00:25:13.330
and having teams
accountable for their costs,

00:25:13.330 --> 00:25:15.100
and regularly checking
these, it actually

00:25:15.100 --> 00:25:16.517
does something
interesting, and it

00:25:16.517 --> 00:25:19.240
makes the culture more
operationally in tune

00:25:19.240 --> 00:25:20.720
with what's going on.

00:25:20.720 --> 00:25:23.607
We've had customers like,
here, Vendasta, Dale Birch,

00:25:23.607 --> 00:25:25.690
say that once they've put
up their cost management

00:25:25.690 --> 00:25:27.850
dashboards, they've identified
things they probably wouldn't

00:25:27.850 --> 00:25:28.780
have identified otherwise.

00:25:28.780 --> 00:25:30.700
So they have a great talk
where they're talking

00:25:30.700 --> 00:25:31.992
about runaway storage spending.

00:25:31.992 --> 00:25:34.680
So usually, storage spending
that runs away slowly

00:25:34.680 --> 00:25:36.430
is something that's
kind of hard to catch.

00:25:36.430 --> 00:25:38.423
And then you might
notice it over months.

00:25:38.423 --> 00:25:40.840
But you would have to actually
look at the long-term trend

00:25:40.840 --> 00:25:43.030
and have someone
proactively look at that.

00:25:43.030 --> 00:25:45.250
But if you just have a
dashboard that says year

00:25:45.250 --> 00:25:47.957
to date spending, and you
see a trend that's going up,

00:25:47.957 --> 00:25:49.540
it's hard to miss,
just because humans

00:25:49.540 --> 00:25:52.240
are so great at identifying
the visual trend.

00:25:52.240 --> 00:25:55.520
And usually, cost is a
great proxy for operations.

00:25:55.520 --> 00:25:57.520
So if there's a
deviation in cost,

00:25:57.520 --> 00:25:59.680
or there's a trend in cost
that doesn't look right,

00:25:59.680 --> 00:26:02.263
but just looks like it deviated
from the past pattern, usually

00:26:02.263 --> 00:26:05.140
that correlates to something
interesting or wrong

00:26:05.140 --> 00:26:05.920
in operations.

00:26:05.920 --> 00:26:07.360
And it's just a
really nice proxy

00:26:07.360 --> 00:26:09.280
that everyone
understands, whether it's

00:26:09.280 --> 00:26:12.770
the VP or the engineer,
that something has started

00:26:12.770 --> 00:26:14.020
to change from the status quo.

00:26:16.726 --> 00:26:21.678
Great, so for the demo, while
Shashank sets that up, what

