00:33:43.190 --> 00:33:46.280
And let's see, Billing reports
demo, that's the project.

00:33:46.280 --> 00:33:49.220
And you know, the budget amount
might be something like $1,000.

00:33:49.220 --> 00:33:50.720
So I don't ask for
more than $1,000.

00:33:50.720 --> 00:33:53.450
I'm comfortable with $1,000
for the demo, for the month,

00:33:53.450 --> 00:33:55.460
but not that much more.

00:33:55.460 --> 00:33:58.370
And then now, the
auto-suggested thresholds

00:33:58.370 --> 00:34:00.500
are, notify me at
50%, 90%, 100%.

00:34:00.500 --> 00:34:03.000
Another interesting thing that
we launched is you could say,

00:34:03.000 --> 00:34:08.180
I would also like to be
notified when I am projected--

00:34:08.180 --> 00:34:09.600
oops, it has to be unique.

00:34:09.600 --> 00:34:12.260
So I'm just going to delete
that and use the last one-- when

00:34:12.260 --> 00:34:15.739
I'm projected or forecasted
to exceed the budget.

00:34:15.739 --> 00:34:18.199
And so you don't have to wait
until you exceed a threshold.

00:34:18.199 --> 00:34:19.820
Just say, when we
forecast that you're

00:34:19.820 --> 00:34:20.989
going to exceed the
budget, even if it's only

00:34:20.989 --> 00:34:23.360
the 10th of the month or
the 15th of the month,

00:34:23.360 --> 00:34:25.800
we should alert on that.

00:34:25.800 --> 00:34:29.000
And then finally, you could
either send that via email

00:34:29.000 --> 00:34:32.572
or connect it to a Pub/Sub
topic if you want to respond

00:34:32.572 --> 00:34:33.530
to it programmatically.

00:34:33.530 --> 00:34:35.330
And because you can
have multiple budgets,

00:34:35.330 --> 00:34:37.139
you could set one this
just sends an email,

00:34:37.139 --> 00:34:39.489
and then set an
identical one then sets--

00:34:39.489 --> 00:34:42.290
that sends a Pub/Sub--

00:34:42.290 --> 00:34:43.489
Pub/Sub alert.

00:34:43.489 --> 00:34:47.114
So I think the topic
is in this project.

00:34:47.114 --> 00:34:48.489
You just have to
select a Pub/Sub

00:34:48.489 --> 00:34:49.860
topic to send the message to.

00:34:49.860 --> 00:34:52.179
So alert email-- save that.

00:34:52.179 --> 00:34:55.900
And then you have a
budget that helps me stay

00:34:55.900 --> 00:34:57.640
in control of my demo spending.

00:35:00.480 --> 00:35:02.440
And then, again,
the Cloud Function

00:35:02.440 --> 00:35:03.680
itself could be very simple.

00:35:03.680 --> 00:35:05.580
So if you're going
into Cloud spending,

00:35:05.580 --> 00:35:08.426
if you go into Cloud Functions--

00:35:12.394 --> 00:35:16.070
oops, I'll just cheat
and go over to the tabs--

00:35:16.070 --> 00:35:19.360
you'll get a list of all the
functions in your project.

00:35:19.360 --> 00:35:21.360
And this one is actually
one that's very simple.

00:35:21.360 --> 00:35:24.500
It responds to any messages
sent to the Pub/Sub

00:35:24.500 --> 00:35:27.195
topic, alert email.

00:35:27.195 --> 00:35:30.310
And in this case, it's a
function that just forwards it.

00:35:30.310 --> 00:35:31.460
It's not truly an email.

00:35:31.460 --> 00:35:34.210
It just forwards it to Slack.

00:35:34.210 --> 00:35:39.630
And that-- if you're using a
standard language like Node.js,

00:35:39.630 --> 00:35:44.990
just include the Slack library,
and then post a message to it.

00:35:44.990 --> 00:35:45.990
So this is a fake token.

00:35:45.990 --> 00:35:48.300
But post a message
to the Slack channel.

00:35:48.300 --> 00:35:49.950
And they'll just
post the contents

00:35:49.950 --> 00:35:50.610
of your Pub/Sub message.

00:35:50.610 --> 00:35:51.693
And that is your function.

00:35:51.693 --> 00:35:53.170
You don't have to
do anything else.

00:35:53.170 --> 00:35:54.195
And when you hit
Deploy, that function

00:35:54.195 --> 00:35:55.945
will start responding
to the budget alert.

00:35:55.945 --> 00:35:57.480
So it's a really powerful tool.

00:35:57.480 --> 00:36:00.390
And you can learn more
about it in a deep dive talk

00:36:00.390 --> 00:36:03.180
that my colleague Phill
Coletti is going to give.

00:36:03.180 --> 00:36:04.700
So now that I've
gone through how

00:36:04.700 --> 00:36:06.797
I control Shashank's
spending, I'll

00:36:06.797 --> 00:36:08.630
let him talk through
billing export and data

00:36:08.630 --> 00:36:10.420
through your dashboards.

00:36:10.420 --> 00:36:12.610
SHASHANK JOSHI: Sure,
thanks, Stephen.

00:36:12.610 --> 00:36:16.362
I really hope you didn't catch
my pet project, but you did.

00:36:16.362 --> 00:36:17.570
So the tools are pretty good.

00:36:21.030 --> 00:36:22.960
So I'm going to talk
about, how will you

00:36:22.960 --> 00:36:25.180
overlay some of the
org data that I spoke

00:36:25.180 --> 00:36:28.450
about in the enterprise
example with your billing data

00:36:28.450 --> 00:36:29.860
and how we can use that.

00:36:29.860 --> 00:36:32.500
So just to set an
example, let's say

00:36:32.500 --> 00:36:34.930
you have multiple LoBs in
your environment, three Lines

00:36:34.930 --> 00:36:38.200
of Businesses, multiple VPs.

00:36:38.200 --> 00:36:41.710
If you see these names familiar,
these are the Docker container

00:36:41.710 --> 00:36:42.610
name generator.

00:36:42.610 --> 00:36:44.800
So these are the VPs.

00:36:44.800 --> 00:36:47.330
And each of the VPs would
have one or more applications.

00:36:47.330 --> 00:36:51.100
And each applications
would have environments.

00:36:51.100 --> 00:36:54.640
And each environment would
be in a specific project.

00:36:54.640 --> 00:36:57.970
And to overlay this structure
with your billing usage,

00:36:57.970 --> 00:37:00.080
you have to have a
labeling strategy.

00:37:00.080 --> 00:37:03.530
So I'm using labels
such as environment--

00:37:03.530 --> 00:37:06.460
so whether it's a
dev, test, prod--

00:37:06.460 --> 00:37:08.800
what lob it is--
lob1, lob2, lob3--

00:37:08.800 --> 00:37:10.030
what app it is--

00:37:10.030 --> 00:37:12.970
whether it's
phill-good, all-is-well.

00:37:12.970 --> 00:37:14.020
And you get the idea.

00:37:14.020 --> 00:37:16.858
We are using some
interesting names here.

00:37:16.858 --> 00:37:19.810
So this is good.

00:37:19.810 --> 00:37:24.280
But how will you really
implement that into BigQuery,

00:37:24.280 --> 00:37:27.810
where you can
create, for example--

00:37:27.810 --> 00:37:30.030
billing export is
where your export is.

00:37:30.030 --> 00:37:33.280
And you can create another
data set for the org data.

00:37:33.280 --> 00:37:35.670
So we have a table
for LoB to VP mapping.

00:37:35.670 --> 00:37:38.340
Let me show you that.

00:37:38.340 --> 00:37:42.020
So it can be your LoB
mapping back to your VP name.

00:37:42.020 --> 00:37:47.450
And we'll see, how will you join
that with your billing export?

00:37:47.450 --> 00:37:51.240
And let's go to the
next level of details.

00:37:51.240 --> 00:37:55.340
So you may have some budgets
for your individual VPs.

00:37:55.340 --> 00:37:59.810
So you can create another
table that maps your VP

00:37:59.810 --> 00:38:01.490
with your month and budget.

00:38:01.490 --> 00:38:04.862
And all of these details
can be stored in a CSV file.

00:38:04.862 --> 00:38:06.570
And you can create a
automation pipeline.

00:38:06.570 --> 00:38:09.530
So whenever there is a-- and you
can check these CSV files back

00:38:09.530 --> 00:38:11.137
into your source
code repository.

00:38:11.137 --> 00:38:12.470
And whenever there is a change--

00:38:12.470 --> 00:38:14.690
let's say a new VP
joins, or a VP leaves,

00:38:14.690 --> 00:38:16.730
or a budget changes
on a month on month--

00:38:16.730 --> 00:38:18.530
you update the CSV file.

00:38:18.530 --> 00:38:22.200
And that automatically
updates your BigQuery table.

00:38:22.200 --> 00:38:26.220
So that's about your org data to
overlay with your billing data.

00:38:26.220 --> 00:38:28.020
Now let's look at some
of the other data.

00:38:28.020 --> 00:38:31.570
I mentioned about SKU categories
as a custom field that we used.

00:38:31.570 --> 00:38:35.160
So that's where we
created a table that

00:38:35.160 --> 00:38:38.380
gets your service, all
your SKUs, and inserts

00:38:38.380 --> 00:38:41.760
a category cards SKU category
just for simplicity there.

00:38:41.760 --> 00:38:44.830
And let me show you
how effective is that.

00:38:44.830 --> 00:38:47.540
So here I am using--

00:38:47.540 --> 00:38:51.817
for example, for Compute Engine,
just if you see the licensing

00:38:51.817 --> 00:38:53.650
itself-- so as I
mentioned, there are, like,

00:38:53.650 --> 00:38:54.910
thousands of services--

00:38:54.910 --> 00:38:55.930
SKUs.

00:38:55.930 --> 00:38:58.420
And within Compute--
just for licensing,

00:38:58.420 --> 00:38:59.890
there are hundreds of SKUs.

00:38:59.890 --> 00:39:02.060
So if you see, there
are-- in this case,

00:39:02.060 --> 00:39:06.130
there are 16 different SKUs in
my usage itself for licensing.

00:39:06.130 --> 00:39:08.200
And for reporting,
I may just want

00:39:08.200 --> 00:39:11.170
to see-- or clip all of them
together as licensing, right?

00:39:11.170 --> 00:39:15.040
So that's what I'm doing here.

00:39:15.040 --> 00:39:16.180
And then let's look at--

00:39:20.030 --> 00:39:22.430
the other important
thing I mentioned

00:39:22.430 --> 00:39:24.600
was about the platform data.

00:39:24.600 --> 00:39:28.370
So in this case, I actually
created a Python script

00:39:28.370 --> 00:39:29.570
using Google APIs.

00:39:29.570 --> 00:39:32.810
What it does is it goes
through all of your products,

00:39:32.810 --> 00:39:35.870
gets all of your instance
data, and dumps it

00:39:35.870 --> 00:39:37.490
into a BigQuery table.

00:39:37.490 --> 00:39:39.350
And you can schedule
this script to run

00:39:39.350 --> 00:39:42.510
every two hours, or every day,
based on what you want to use.

00:39:42.510 --> 00:39:43.800
And this is just one example.

00:39:43.800 --> 00:39:46.040
The API is so
enormous and useful,

00:39:46.040 --> 00:39:48.380
you can write as
many custom scripts.

00:39:48.380 --> 00:39:51.830
So this particular
script gets the details

00:39:51.830 --> 00:39:55.370
of the project it's running
in, what instance and name,

00:39:55.370 --> 00:39:57.800
what region and zone
it's running in,

00:39:57.800 --> 00:40:00.540
and how many cores and RAM
does the instance have.

00:40:00.540 --> 00:40:03.600
And we'll see how
that data is used.

00:40:03.600 --> 00:40:06.170
And because we are using
project labels to map

00:40:06.170 --> 00:40:09.290
to line of business, now you
can map all of these instances

00:40:09.290 --> 00:40:12.540
back to your VP.

00:40:12.540 --> 00:40:14.703
So now we have
created the structure.

00:40:14.703 --> 00:40:15.870
So there is a billing table.

00:40:15.870 --> 00:40:17.578
And there are a bunch
of different tables

00:40:17.578 --> 00:40:19.292
for your org data,
for your other data.

00:40:19.292 --> 00:40:21.000
Now let's see how we
can join all of this

00:40:21.000 --> 00:40:23.790
to make some
meaningful dashboards.

00:40:23.790 --> 00:40:25.860
So for that, we create
a bunch of views.

00:40:25.860 --> 00:40:27.000
Let's look at those views.

00:40:30.900 --> 00:40:35.940
So the first view that you see
here is a billing daily view.

00:40:35.940 --> 00:40:38.440
So here, what we are
joining here is--

00:40:41.450 --> 00:40:44.630
I hope the text is visible.

00:40:44.630 --> 00:40:47.200
So we are joining the
main building table

00:40:47.200 --> 00:40:49.870
with the SKU categories
and with the LoB mapping.

00:40:49.870 --> 00:40:52.000
So what you get
as a result is you

00:40:52.000 --> 00:40:54.445
can map your billing
usage by your VP

00:40:54.445 --> 00:40:55.490
and by the SKU category.

00:40:55.490 --> 00:40:56.770
So you're merging these--

00:40:56.770 --> 00:40:59.720
joining these--
the three tables.

00:40:59.720 --> 00:41:02.230
The other view is the
billing monthly budget view.

00:41:02.230 --> 00:41:06.880
So in this view, you're
joining your billing table

00:41:06.880 --> 00:41:13.722
with your LoBs as well
as your monthly budget.

00:41:13.722 --> 00:41:15.680
So that's how you are
joining these two tables.

00:41:15.680 --> 00:41:18.380
And finally, the
instance data and VP--

00:41:18.380 --> 00:41:20.160
so in this particular
view, you're

00:41:20.160 --> 00:41:25.980
joining the instance data with
the VP table and the LoB table.

00:41:25.980 --> 00:41:28.260
So this is just an
example to set it up,

00:41:28.260 --> 00:41:30.510
how you can join
different tables together

00:41:30.510 --> 00:41:32.490
with your billing export.

00:41:32.490 --> 00:41:35.250
So you created the
tables, additional tables.

00:41:35.250 --> 00:41:36.810
Of course, you had
a billing export.

00:41:36.810 --> 00:41:39.300
Then you need to
create a bunch of views

00:41:39.300 --> 00:41:42.040
on how to join these
different tables.

00:41:42.040 --> 00:41:44.970
And then finally, you
create the dashboards.

00:41:44.970 --> 00:41:46.920
And as Stephen mentioned,
it's really easy

00:41:46.920 --> 00:41:50.340
to do Data Studio dashboards.

00:41:50.340 --> 00:41:52.770
If you just search on GCP
Billing Data Studio sample

00:41:52.770 --> 00:41:55.050
on Google, you'll see that.

00:41:55.050 --> 00:41:56.700
You'll see Stephen's photo here.

00:41:56.700 --> 00:41:59.220
I think it's a really
nice talk from last year--

00:41:59.220 --> 00:42:02.860
so a must-see, must
see your YouTube video.

00:42:02.860 --> 00:42:06.690
So what you do is you just
click on this one, this link.

00:42:06.690 --> 00:42:09.750
And it takes you to a page here.

00:42:09.750 --> 00:42:12.900
And there are details given on
how to-- how Data Studio works.

00:42:12.900 --> 00:42:18.135
But more interestingly, there is
start working with your sample

00:42:18.135 --> 00:42:18.760
billing report.

00:42:18.760 --> 00:42:21.020
So you just click on this link.

00:42:21.020 --> 00:42:23.480
That takes you to
a sample dashboard.

00:42:23.480 --> 00:42:27.710
So that gives you, out of the
box, many dashboards, right?

00:42:27.710 --> 00:42:31.850
Your analysis by projects,
analysis by resources, spending

00:42:31.850 --> 00:42:34.190
trends, App Engine, BigQuery--
all of these reports

00:42:34.190 --> 00:42:36.180
are available to you.

00:42:36.180 --> 00:42:38.860
And it's so easy to use
in your environment.

00:42:38.860 --> 00:42:43.650
You just click on Make a copy,
and give your data source,

00:42:43.650 --> 00:42:44.800
your billing export here.

00:42:44.800 --> 00:42:47.100
So in my case, it's
Billing_Export_Table.

00:42:47.100 --> 00:42:48.810
I just click on Copy report.

00:42:48.810 --> 00:42:50.650
And all those
dashboards you see,

00:42:50.650 --> 00:42:56.330
those that are available for
my environment out of the box.

00:42:56.330 --> 00:42:59.510
So this is how easier
it is to really do this.

00:42:59.510 --> 00:43:03.190
So this is-- this can give
you out-of-the-box reports.

00:43:03.190 --> 00:43:05.530
But how to make some of the
custom reports that we saw?

00:43:05.530 --> 00:43:08.960
So many tables, so many views--
how do you consume that?

00:43:08.960 --> 00:43:14.500
So let's go to the next cost
management custom reports--

00:43:14.500 --> 00:43:17.980
and a shout out to David Wright,
who helped, creating some

00:43:17.980 --> 00:43:19.180
of these reports and views.

00:43:23.620 --> 00:43:26.630
So the first one-- because you
saw the first view that we had

00:43:26.630 --> 00:43:31.670
was combining the VP and LoB
table with our billing export.

00:43:31.670 --> 00:43:32.750
And because of that--

00:43:32.750 --> 00:43:37.830
let me increase
the font size here.

00:43:37.830 --> 00:43:41.420
So because of that, you could
see the spending by the VPs.

00:43:41.420 --> 00:43:44.180
So you can see that Amazing
Aryabhata is spending

00:43:44.180 --> 00:43:46.610
most of the money for you.

00:43:46.610 --> 00:43:48.320
The other insight
that you see here

00:43:48.320 --> 00:43:52.470
is there are some
unassigned costs as well.

00:43:52.470 --> 00:43:55.520
So these are the projects
which you have not labeled.

00:43:55.520 --> 00:43:59.510
And it's really important, as
organization, for you to see,

00:43:59.510 --> 00:44:02.133
what are my costs that are not
labelled, not allocated, right?

00:44:02.133 --> 00:44:03.175
So it's really important.

00:44:03.175 --> 00:44:04.640
And it's clearly
visible from here.

00:44:07.420 --> 00:44:09.762
Now let's look at-- we had
budgets for everybody, right?

00:44:09.762 --> 00:44:11.220
So let's look at
how they are doing

00:44:11.220 --> 00:44:12.762
vis-a-vis the budgets
that they have.

00:44:12.762 --> 00:44:14.820
So this is the view where
we combine the budgets

00:44:14.820 --> 00:44:16.810
table with the billing table.

00:44:16.810 --> 00:44:20.240
So you can see, for example,
Relaxed Richie is our VP.

00:44:20.240 --> 00:44:25.450
He's below our budget, so he
can continue to stay relax.

00:44:25.450 --> 00:44:29.070
Let's look at somebody else.

00:44:29.070 --> 00:44:32.250
So Mr. Trusting Torvalds is
actually exceeding his budget.

00:44:32.250 --> 00:44:35.050
So we'll have to have
a discussion with him,

00:44:35.050 --> 00:44:36.750
and why is that.

00:44:36.750 --> 00:44:39.697
So you can see what they're
doing vis-a-vis daily budget

00:44:39.697 --> 00:44:40.530
and monthly as well.

00:44:40.530 --> 00:44:45.970
You see daily spend, monthly
spend, vis-a-vis their budgets.

00:44:45.970 --> 00:44:49.652
Coming back to the
SKU categories,

00:44:49.652 --> 00:44:51.110
because these are
categories, these

00:44:51.110 --> 00:44:52.710
are much higher-level
abstractions.

00:44:52.710 --> 00:44:55.860
So you see licensing fees here
as the second biggest cost

00:44:55.860 --> 00:44:56.360
driver.

00:44:56.360 --> 00:44:57.735
That gives you a
really good view

00:44:57.735 --> 00:44:59.120
of where your cost is going.

00:44:59.120 --> 00:45:02.000
So instance VCPU is
definitely number one,

00:45:02.000 --> 00:45:03.590
but licensing is number two.

00:45:03.590 --> 00:45:05.030
So you would want to drill down.

00:45:05.030 --> 00:45:06.860
And as Stephen saw--

00:45:06.860 --> 00:45:09.080
I mean, he caught me
with my pet project--

00:45:09.080 --> 00:45:11.570
that is a bunch of Windows
machines, Windows licensing

00:45:11.570 --> 00:45:13.860
that had licenses as well.

00:45:13.860 --> 00:45:18.124
So you may want to look at
that as cost optimization.

00:45:18.124 --> 00:45:23.750
And finally, let's look at
some of the custom GCP scripts.

00:45:23.750 --> 00:45:27.230
So we had the custom GCP script
getting all the instance data,

00:45:27.230 --> 00:45:31.850
and how that is useful combining
with your other org data.

00:45:35.510 --> 00:45:38.890
So now you can see, by each
VP, how many different types

00:45:38.890 --> 00:45:40.600
of instances they
are running, right?

00:45:40.600 --> 00:45:44.110
So why it is useful,
you may be running

00:45:44.110 --> 00:45:46.690
a lot of-- your developers
may be running a lot of custom

00:45:46.690 --> 00:45:47.560
instances, right?

00:45:47.560 --> 00:45:49.390
So that is an
optimization opportunity

00:45:49.390 --> 00:45:51.400
where you would go to
the standard instance

00:45:51.400 --> 00:45:53.690
and save some cost.

00:45:53.690 --> 00:45:57.050
Instances per zone, this may
be important from availability

00:45:57.050 --> 00:45:58.760
perspective if you're
oversubscribing

00:45:58.760 --> 00:46:00.390
one zone or another.

00:46:00.390 --> 00:46:04.540
For example, in this case,
Amazing Aryabhata is going to--

00:46:04.540 --> 00:46:09.200
his team is prioritizing
one zone over others.

00:46:09.200 --> 00:46:12.700
And these dashboards of cores
and RAM per zone, these details

00:46:12.700 --> 00:46:15.100
are really useful
for you to make

00:46:15.100 --> 00:46:17.620
informed decision on
how many commitments

00:46:17.620 --> 00:46:21.910
you want to buy on each of the
zones-- or each of the regions.

00:46:21.910 --> 00:46:24.480
And finally, whether--
instance status--

00:46:24.480 --> 00:46:26.310
you can do it like,
running, terminated.

00:46:26.310 --> 00:46:29.100
How many instances which
just shut down and just

00:46:29.100 --> 00:46:29.880
left like that?

00:46:29.880 --> 00:46:32.698
You would want to kill
those or delete those.

00:46:32.698 --> 00:46:34.490
This is just example--
just a few examples.

00:46:34.490 --> 00:46:36.840
But you can make as
many custom reports

00:46:36.840 --> 00:46:39.330
and combine that together.

00:46:39.330 --> 00:46:41.370
STEPHEN ELLIOTT: OK,
thanks, Shashank.

00:46:41.370 --> 00:46:43.050
And can we switch
back to the slides?

00:46:45.714 --> 00:46:48.650
All right, so just
to wrap up, we

00:46:48.650 --> 00:46:49.900
mentioned some best practices.

00:46:49.900 --> 00:46:51.860
These slides will be
available online later.

00:46:51.860 --> 00:46:55.798
But these are worth reiterating,
which is, on day one,

00:46:55.798 --> 00:46:58.090
you've seen the value of
having billing export enabled.

00:46:58.090 --> 00:47:01.447
So you should enable that when
you have a billing account.

00:47:01.447 --> 00:47:03.280
And that way, that
historical data is always

00:47:03.280 --> 00:47:04.718
available to you.

00:47:04.718 --> 00:47:06.760
And in my demo, I showed
you how reports are just

00:47:06.760 --> 00:47:10.060
a very easy tool to
use to quickly find

00:47:10.060 --> 00:47:12.407
a date range and a product
or SKU of interest,

00:47:12.407 --> 00:47:14.240
and then use that as
your jumping-off point,

00:47:14.240 --> 00:47:16.460
whether it's Stackdriver
or the detailed logs.

00:47:16.460 --> 00:47:19.110
And that exploration,
if you know the tools,

00:47:19.110 --> 00:47:20.860
can take a matter of
minutes to figure out

00:47:20.860 --> 00:47:23.500
what's driving something.

00:47:23.500 --> 00:47:25.630
Finally, it's really worth
putting in some effort

00:47:25.630 --> 00:47:27.070
to think about how
you organize your work

00:47:27.070 --> 00:47:29.278
and how you want to manage
your costs to build custom

00:47:29.278 --> 00:47:31.943
dashboards off of that from
the detailed billing data

00:47:31.943 --> 00:47:33.610
so that you can spread
those dashboards.

00:47:33.610 --> 00:47:36.170
Data Studio lets you
share them easily.

00:47:36.170 --> 00:47:39.020
So you can share those
dashboards with your company.

00:47:39.020 --> 00:47:42.775
And then related to that is just
developing your cost management

00:47:42.775 --> 00:47:45.400
strategy and thinking about how
you're organizing the resources

00:47:45.400 --> 00:47:47.880
and the projects, how you
want to label cross-cutting

00:47:47.880 --> 00:47:50.380
spend across different projects
and different resource types

00:47:50.380 --> 00:47:53.560
so that you can easily see
fully-loaded application costs

00:47:53.560 --> 00:47:55.360
or fully-loaded team costs.

00:47:55.360 --> 00:47:58.120
And then finally, if you do all
this right, just really work

00:47:58.120 --> 00:48:01.450
at building that culture of cost
ownership, making sure people

00:48:01.450 --> 00:48:03.760
actually use the cost
dashboards and our iteratively

00:48:03.760 --> 00:48:06.220
improving it, as Shashank
was mentioning earlier,

00:48:06.220 --> 00:48:08.753
so that they both get the cost
